From d46499e42655e9927f54a258cbec560761de9dcd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Velle Sinclair <brncomputerhelp@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 16:26:20 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 39/42] feat(render): layer properties' timeline animations
 play

The editor keyframes a property on the value itself:

    "alpha": { "value": 1, "animation": { "c0": [...], "options":
               { "fps": 30, "length": 120, "mode": "single" } } }

Nothing read "animation", so each property sat at its static value.
In the library that is 37 properties in 10 wallpapers: layers that
should fade in at load stayed invisible (six in each of the two Music
Dialogue wallpapers), a cover that should fade out after four seconds
stayed, and origins, light intensities and scales that pulse or drift
stood still.

The parser reads each curve (c0 to c3, one per component) with its
keyframes and handles, and the loop, mirror or single mode, frame
rate, length, startpaused and relative options. A scriptable layer
plays each animated property's curve into it every frame, before the
scripts that read it run. Its clock starts the first time it plays,
so an intro runs from the start whenever the wallpaper loads. Between
keyframes the curve is a cubic Bezier. A handle's x is in thirds of
its segment, so the editor's default flat handles give a smoothstep
ease and a segment without handles is straight. A relative animation
adds to the value the editor placed.

Scripts reach an animation through ILayer.getAnimation (name), and
with no name the one on the script's own property, as the media
widgets do (`thisObject.getAnimation ().play ()` on a track change).
IAnimation answers what ITextureAnimation does (play, pause, stop,
isPlaying, getFrame, setFrame, frameCount, duration, rate) plus name
and fps, through the same functions.

